  {
   "id": "auS9PABS8V0J",
   "from": "yrosseel",
   "author": "89c9fc822e34b78c",
   "date": "2012-11-26T19:18:08Z",
   "subject": "Re: Change score regression: Impossible in lavaan v.5?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "On 11/26/2012 05:26 PM, Robert Miller wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hello everyone,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; very recently i encountered a problem, which was not present in lavaan v.4.\r<br>\r<br>could you be more specific: which version of lavaan v4? (I tried 0.4-10 \r<br>and 0.4-14, but got the same results as 0.5-10).\r<br>\r<br>&gt; Specifically, i tried model a change score regression. However,\r<br>&gt; predicted change scores deviate from the &quot;true&quot; change scores:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; /library(lavaan)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; *#generate data*\r<br>&gt; x &lt;- rnorm(150,5,1)\r<br>&gt; change &lt;- rnorm(150,2,1)\r<br>&gt; y &lt;- x + change\r<br>&gt; df &lt;- data.frame(x,y,change)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; *#specify model*\r<br>&gt; mod_lc &lt;- &quot;\r<br>&gt; #latent change regression\r<br>&gt; change =~ 1*y\r<br>&gt; change ~ x\r<br>&gt; #mean structure\r<br>&gt; x ~ 1\r<br>&gt; y ~ 0\r<br>&gt; change ~ 1\r<br>&gt; #variances\r<br>&gt; x ~~ x\r<br>&gt; change ~~ change\r<br>&gt; &quot;\r<br>&gt; *\r<br>&gt; #fit model and compare change scores*\r<br>&gt; fit_lc &lt;- lavaan(mod_lc, orthogonal=T, fixed.x=F, auto.fix.single = T,\r<br>&gt; data=df[,1:2])\r<br>&gt; summary(fit_lc, fit.measures=T, standardized=T, rsquare=T)\r<br>&gt; head(round(cbind(predict(fit_lc), df[,3]),3))/\r<br>\r<br>Hm. As you specify your model, &#39;change&#39; is set equal to &#39;y&#39;. So, AFAICS, \r<br>predict() will give scores that are equal to &#39;change + x&#39;, but not &#39;change&#39;:\r<br>\r<br>fit.lm &lt;- lm(y ~ x, data=df)\r<br>head(cbind(predict(fit_lc), df$x + df$change))\r<br>\r<br>&gt; To my mind, this is likely to be due to the fixed.x - argument, which\r<br>&gt; somehow causes lavaan v.5 (in contrast to lavaan v.4) to estimate mean\r<br>&gt; and variance of /x/ instead of fixing it to the sample value. Does\r<br>&gt; anyone has an idea how to solve this problem?\r<br>\r<br>As far as I know, the behavior of the fixed.x argument has not changed \r<br>between v4 and v5. If fixed.x=TRUE (and you remove both x~1 and x~~x \r<br>from your syntax!), the mean/variance of x is fixed at its sample value. \r<br>But this does not affect the predict() values.\r<br>\r<br>Yves.\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
